WEBVTT

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:02.680
<v Hannah Jacoby>This is Hannah.
<v Hannah Jacoby>这是汉娜。

00:00:02.680 --> 00:00:04.960
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, hi, Hannah. This is Sarah from This American Life.
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，嗨，汉娜。我是《美国生活》节目的莎拉。

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:05.800
<v Hannah Jacoby>Hi, how are you?
<v Hannah Jacoby>嗨，你好吗？

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:07.510
<v Sarah Koenig>I'm good, thank you. How are you?
<v Sarah Koenig>我很好，谢谢。你呢？

00:00:07.510 --> 00:00:08.620
<v Hannah Jacoby>Good.
<v Hannah Jacoby>很好。

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:21.630
<v Sarah Koenig>Before we go any further, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Sarah Koenig, sitting in for Ira Glass. And you're about to hear this story the same way I did-- blind. All I know is that it's going to have a coincidence in it.
<v Sarah Koenig>在继续深入之前，这是来自芝加哥WBEZ电台的《美国生活》。我是莎拉·凯尼格，代班艾拉·格拉斯。你们即将听到这个故事，就像我当时听到它时一样——完全不知情。我只知道它里面会有一个巧合。

00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:25.780
<v Sarah Koenig>I have no idea what you're about to tell me, so lay it on me.
<v Sarah Koenig>我完全不知道你要告诉我什么，所以请讲吧。

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:32.440
<v Hannah Jacoby>So the story is, I was a freshman in high school at Bozeman High School--
<v Hannah Jacoby>故事是这样的，我高中一年级的时候在博兹曼高中——

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.730
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, in Montana.
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，在蒙大拿州。

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:50.770
<v Hannah Jacoby>Yes. And I was in the orchestra. And it was early in my freshman year and I had made friends in high school, but didn't really have orchestra friends yet. And we were taking an orchestra trip. So that was a little nerve-wracking.
<v Hannah Jacoby>是的。我是管弦乐队的成员。那是在高一刚开学不久，我在学校里交到了朋友，但还没有真正成为管弦乐队的朋友。当时我们要去参加一个管弦乐队的旅行，所以我有点紧张。

00:00:50.770 --> 00:01:05.410
<v Sarah Koenig>When you're a freshman, this qualifies as a demi-crisis. A long bus ride to Seattle, sharing a hotel room with someone you don't know. But there's this one girl, Lindsay. She and Hannah have friends in common so they decide to pair up-- to room together, to ride the bus together, to be orchestra friends.
<v Sarah Koenig>当你还是个大一新生时，这就算是一场小型危机了。坐长途大巴去西雅图，和一个不认识的人合住酒店房间。但有个叫琳赛的女孩，她和汉娜有共同的朋友，所以她们决定搭档——住在一起，坐同一辆大巴，成为管弦乐队的朋友。

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:13.210
<v Hannah Jacoby>So we go to Seattle and we're like, it's awkward because we're new friends, trying to get to know each other.
<v Hannah Jacoby>所以我们去了西雅图，感觉很尴尬，因为我们是新朋友，还在努力了解对方。

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:14.050
<v Sarah Koenig>Right.
<v Sarah Koenig>没错。

00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:19.660
<v Hannah Jacoby>And on one of our free days they send us to the Space Needle and I don't know if you've ever been to the Space Needle--
<v Hannah Jacoby>在我们其中一个自由活动日，他们让我们去太空针塔，不知道你有没有去过太空针塔——

00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:20.290
<v Sarah Koenig>I have.
<v Sarah Koenig>我去过。

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:30.940
<v Hannah Jacoby>But there's that carnival area all around it. And we are playing the little games all around there and we won Poopatroopers. Do you know what those are?
<v Hannah Jacoby>它周围有一个嘉年华区域。我们在那里玩各种小游戏，赢了一些“便便伞兵”（Poopatroopers）。你知道那是什么吗？

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:31.980
<v Sarah Koenig>No.
<v Sarah Koenig>不知道。

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:43.540
<v Hannah Jacoby>OK. They're the little plastic soldiers that have a plastic parachute. You're supposed to drop them from somewhere and the plastic parachute expands and--
<v Hannah Jacoby>好吧。那是些带有塑料降落伞的小塑料士兵。你应该把它们从某处扔下去，塑料降落伞就会张开，然后——

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.300
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, what did you call them?
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，你叫它们什么？

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:47.230
<v Hannah Jacoby>Poopatroopers?
<v Hannah Jacoby>便便伞兵？

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.180
<v Sarah Koenig>Really?
<v Sarah Koenig>真的吗？

00:01:49.180 --> 00:01:54.190
<v Hannah Jacoby>I didn't quite believe Hannah about this, but she looked it up. It's true. Poopatroopers.
<v Hannah Jacoby>关于这一点，我起初不太相信汉娜，但她查了一下。是真的，确实叫“便便伞兵”。

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:59.790
<v Hannah Jacoby>I just did a Google image search for Poopatroopers and I'm seeing images of the little guys.
<v Hannah Jacoby>我刚在谷歌图片上搜索了“Poopatroopers”，我确实看到了那些小家伙的图片。

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:02.380
<v Sarah Koenig>But wait. Is it a generic name or is it the brand name?
<v Sarah Koenig>等等，这是一个通用名称还是品牌名称？

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:08.410
<v Hannah Jacoby>I think it's a brand name. I think, from what I'm seeing here, Imperial Poopatroopers, maybe?
<v Hannah Jacoby>我想是品牌名。从我看到的这些来看，可能是“帝国便便伞兵”？

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:09.330
<v Hannah Jacoby>[SARAH LAUGHS]
<v Hannah Jacoby>[莎拉大笑]

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:13.010
<v Sarah Koenig>His Highness? Four star general Poopatrooper?
<v Sarah Koenig>殿下？四星将军便便伞兵？

00:02:13.010 --> 00:02:16.390
<v Hannah Jacoby>Yes. Exactly.
<v Hannah Jacoby>是的，完全正确。

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:22.210
<v Sarah Koenig>Back to the plot here. Hannah and her new friend Lindsay go to the top of the Seattle Space Needle to try out their prizes.
<v Sarah Koenig>回到正题。汉娜和她的新朋友琳赛来到西雅图太空针塔的顶部去试玩她们的奖品。

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:41.010
<v Hannah Jacoby>So we got in the little elevator and ran to the top and ran to the edge and threw the Poopatroopers over the edge. Because there's nothing to stop you, really. And we were just in peals of laughter and it was-- we thought it was so hilarious. And it sort of broke the ice in our friendship.
<v Hannah Jacoby>我们进了小电梯，跑到顶端，冲到边缘，把便便伞兵扔了下去。因为那里真的没有任何阻挡。我们当时笑得前仰后合，真的——我们觉得太搞笑了。这在某种程度上打破了我们友谊的僵局。

00:02:41.010 --> 00:02:52.240
<v Sarah Koenig>Lindsay and Hannah become best friends all through high school. Cut to senior year-- four years later-- another orchestra trip. This time to Reno, Nevada. Hannah and Lindsay are in their hotel room studying.
<v Sarah Koenig>琳赛和汉娜在高中成了最好的朋友。时间跳转到高四——四年后——又是另一次管弦乐队的旅行。这次是去内华达州的里诺。汉娜和琳赛在酒店房间里学习。

00:02:52.240 --> 00:03:16.450
<v Hannah Jacoby>And so Lindsay and I were both sitting at the desk in this hotel room in Reno, Nevada and a Poopatrooper comes flying out of the sky and lands on the windowsill of our hotel room where we are-- and we both-- we couldn't believe it. We opened the window and we grabbed the Poopatrooper. And we ran into the hall to try to find other people in our orchestra.
<v Hannah Jacoby>琳赛和我都坐在内华达州里诺酒店房间的书桌前，一个便便伞兵从天而降，落在了我们房间的窗台上——我们俩都——简直不敢相信。我们打开窗户，抓住了那个便便伞兵。然后我们跑到走廊里，试图寻找管弦乐队里的其他人。

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:33.820
<v Hannah Jacoby>We thought someone had been playing a trick on us. But no one knew what we were talking about. No one even remembered the thing in Seattle. And so I, still to this day, don't know why that happened.
<v Hannah Jacoby>我们以为有人在恶作剧。但没人知道我们在说什么。甚至没人记得在西雅图发生过那件事。直到今天，我仍然不知道那为什么会发生。

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:47.560
<v Sarah Koenig>Every once in a while, we get pitches like this at the radio show-- coincidence stories. Since they don't really fit into our usual every week we bring you a theme format, we've been setting them aside wondering what to do with them. They mean so much to people.
<v Sarah Koenig>偶尔，我们会收到像这样的投稿——巧合故事。因为它们不太符合我们每周带给你的主题格式，我们一直把它们放在一边，不知道该怎么办。但它们对人们意义重大。

00:03:47.560 --> 00:04:09.370
<v Hannah Jacoby>I don't know. It felt like it was about our friendship. It felt-- I'm not someone that believes in God, really, but it felt a little bit like an act of God or something just cosmic and larger than us.
<v Hannah Jacoby>我不知道。这感觉就像是关于我们的友谊。这感觉——我并不是一个真正相信上帝的人，但它感觉有点像上帝的作为，或者某种宇宙层面的、比我们更宏大的事物。

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:27.760
<v Sarah Koenig>Finally, it hit us. If we got enough of them, we could put them on the radio as their own thing. So we put out a call to listeners-- send us your coincidence stories. And you did. Hundreds and hundreds of you did. We read at least 1,300 coincidence stories, interviewed dozens of people. Here are just a few things we've learned.
<v Sarah Koenig>最终，我们想通了。如果我们收集到足够多的故事，就可以把它们作为独立内容在广播里播出。于是我们向听众发出了征集——寄给我们你们的巧合故事。你们真的做了。成百上千的人寄来了。我们阅读了至少1300个巧合故事，采访了几十个人。以下是我们学到的一些东西。

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:37.690
<v Sarah Koenig>You're most prone to coincidences when you're young-- teens or 20s. Lots of coincidences involve your grandmother. You're likely to experience a coincidence if you're in or near the Netherlands.
<v Sarah Koenig>当你年轻时——十几岁或二十几岁——最容易遇到巧合。很多巧合都涉及你的祖母。如果你在荷兰境内或附近，很可能会经历巧合。

00:04:37.690 --> 00:04:55.800
<v Sarah Koenig>Coincidences also happen to you in or near airplanes or on Craigslist. For many of you, coincidences were the answer to your prayers or helped you find your missing bike. Also, there are coincidences hidden in your name, your keys, your clothes, your address. Like when Flora and Ragaciano Pena first started dating.
<v Sarah Koenig>巧合也会发生在飞机上、飞机附近或分类广告网站上。对你们中的许多人来说，巧合是你们祈祷的答案，或者帮助你们找回了丢失的自行车。此外，在你的名字、钥匙、衣服、地址中也隐藏着巧合。就像弗洛拉和拉加西亚诺·佩纳刚开始约会时那样。

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:13.930
<v Flora Pena>When we met, he said that he was born in [INAUDIBLE]. And I said, well, I was, too. And then when we were going to get married and we needed our birth certificates, we compared them and it was the same building.
<v Flora Pena>我们见面时，他说他出生在[听不清]。我说，我也是。后来我们要结婚需要出生证明时，我们比较了一下，发现是在同一栋楼。

00:05:13.930 --> 00:05:42.160
<v Sarah Koenig>We heard stories about a lost roll of film discovered, coincidentally, in the stomach of a decomposing hyena. About the man who got his favorite t-shirt back from a stranger's knapsack in Slovakia. About the woman who was dating not one, but two Mark Steven Andersons at the same time. About the student whose car broke down in rush hour on a busy highway and the one person who just happens to stop who comes to her rescue-- it's her dad who lives four hours away.
<v Sarah Koenig>我们听说了关于一卷丢失的胶卷巧合地在一只腐烂的鬣狗胃里被发现的故事；关于那个在斯洛伐克从陌生人的背包里找回自己最喜欢的T恤的男人；关于那个同时和两个名字都叫马克·史蒂文·安德森的人约会的女人；关于那个在繁忙高速公路高峰期车坏了的学生，而唯一停下来救援的人恰好是她住在一小时四小时车程外的父亲。

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:53.020
<v Sarah Koenig>And one of my favorites, from a woman named Jill Peterson, who felt nauseated on the subway in New York City. As they pull into the Second Avenue stop, there's a garbage can, perfectly positioned to help her out.
<v Sarah Koenig>还有一个我最喜欢的，来自一个叫吉尔·彼得森的女人，她在纽约地铁里感到恶心。当车进入第二大道站时，有一个垃圾桶，位置正好可以帮到她。

00:05:53.020 --> 00:06:12.430
<v Jill Peterson>So the train doors open and I'm beelining it for this can. And as I get there, out of the corner of my eye, I see another person heading my direction-- going for the can, too. And it's all coming up and I'm throwing up.
<v Jill Peterson>车门一开，我就直奔那个桶。就在我快到时，我眼角瞥见另一个人也在往我这个方向走——也是奔着桶去的。一切都要喷出来了，我开始呕吐。

00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:26.170
<v Jill Peterson>And as I'm throwing up in this can, this person who's coming towards me starts throwing up in the can, too. And we're both simultaneously barfing into this can in the subway station. And we look at each other like, whoa.
<v Jill Peterson>就在我往桶里吐的时候，那个朝我走来的人也开始往桶里吐。我们俩同时在地铁站的这个桶里呕吐。我们看着对方，心想，哇。

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:34.170
<v Jill Peterson>I don't know. How are you feeling? She's like, oh, I'm feeling a little bit better now. I'm like, me, too.
<v Jill Peterson>我不知道。你感觉怎么样？她说，哦，我现在感觉好多了。我说，我也是。

00:06:34.170 --> 00:06:53.580
<v Sarah Koenig>What's the only way to survive throwing up in front of a subway car full of strangers? Have a nice girl your age do it with you. And numbers-- so many coincidences with numbers. One woman told us how she was at Thanksgiving with her extended family and they suddenly realized that six of them had the exact same number of letters in their names-- 23 letters.
<v Sarah Koenig>在满车陌生人面前呕吐，唯一的生存方式是什么？找个同龄的漂亮女孩和你一起吐。还有数字——关于数字的巧合太多了。一位女士告诉我们，她感恩节和大家庭在一起时，突然意识到他们中有六个人的名字里的字母数量完全相同——都是23个字母。

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:59.470
<v Catherine Van De Putte>Michael Arthur van de Putte, Mary Elizabeth van de Putte, William Arthur van de Putte--
<v Catherine Van De Putte>迈克尔·亚瑟·范德普特，玛丽·伊丽莎白·范德普特，威廉·亚瑟·范德普特——

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:10.650
<v Sarah Koenig>There was the guy at the mall doing surveys who had to ask people their phone number. It's obvious the guy he's surveying starts to make up a number. He says, 933- 45-- 6--
<v Sarah Koenig>还有一个在商场做调查的家伙，必须问人们的电话号码。显而易见，被调查的那个家伙开始胡编一个号码。他说，933-45--6--

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:20.520
<v Man>When he read out that last number, he said, 8? And I stepped back for a second, but I was cool. And I said, no, sir. I'm sorry. That's not your phone number.
<v Man>当他念出最后一个数字时，他说，8？我退后了一步，但我很冷静。我说，不，先生。对不起。那不是你的电话号码。

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:35.660
<v Man>And he said, why isn't that my phone number? And I said, because that's my phone number. That's my phone number. The phone number that you just made up is actually my phone number.
<v Man>他说，为什么那不是我的电话号码？我说，因为那是我的电话号码。那是我的电话号码。你刚才胡编的那个电话号码实际上是我的电话号码。

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:43.670
<v Sarah Koenig>These stories reminded me of an old Chinese saying I once heard. Actually, I learned it a couple of weeks ago and I have no idea how old it is. But it goes like this.
<v Sarah Koenig>这些故事让我想起了一句我曾经听过的古老中国谚语。其实，我是几周前才学到的，我完全不知道它有多古老。它是这样说的。

00:07:43.670 --> 00:07:45.470
<v Yuen Yuen Di>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Yuen Yuen Di>[说普通话]

00:07:45.470 --> 00:07:47.070
<v Sarah Koenig>That's my friend, Yuen Yuen Di.
<v Sarah Koenig>那是我的朋友，袁元娣。

00:07:47.070 --> 00:07:49.260
<v Yuen Yuen Di>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Yuen Yuen Di>[说普通话]

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:51.650
<v Sarah Koenig>And what does it mean?
<v Sarah Koenig>那是什么意思？

00:07:51.650 --> 00:07:54.740
<v Yuen Yuen Di>If there's no coincidence, there won't be stories.
<v Yuen Yuen Di>无巧不成书。

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:57.160
<v Sarah Koenig>No coincidence, no story.
<v Sarah Koenig>没有巧合，就没有故事。

00:07:57.160 --> 00:08:05.810
<v Yuen Yuen Di>To imply that of course, lives are all-- I don't know-- developed in ways that we can't predict.
<v Yuen Yuen Di>意思是，当然，生活的发展方式是我们无法预测的。

00:08:05.810 --> 00:08:11.560
<v Sarah Koenig>So is it like, if you heard a story with a coincidence, afterwards, you might be like, no coincidence, no story-- like that?
<v Sarah Koenig>所以，如果听到一个有巧合的故事，事后你会说，“无巧不成书”——是这样吗？

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.490
<v Yuen Yuen Di>That's right. Yes. Just an expression you use that you quote.
<v Yuen Yuen Di>没错。是的。这只是你引用的一个表达。

00:08:15.490 --> 00:08:17.660
<v Sarah Koenig>Is it a corny thing to say?
<v Sarah Koenig>说这句话会显得很老土吗？

00:08:17.660 --> 00:08:20.930
<v Yuen Yuen Di>Mostly, I think no. It won't be a corny thing to say.
<v Yuen Yuen Di>大部分情况下，我认为不会。说这句话不会显得老土。

00:08:20.930 --> 00:08:24.090
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you ever use it?
<v Sarah Koenig>你用过吗？

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:29.540
<v Yuen Yuen Di>When I'm corny, yes.
<v Yuen Yuen Di>当我表现得老土时，会用。

00:08:29.540 --> 00:09:24.920
<v Sarah Koenig>At the risk of being corny, I'm going to say it. Today's show-- no coincidence, no story. While the big man's away, we're going to sneak these coincidence stories onto the air. All the stories you'll hear today first aired on our show in 2013. And they're all verified true stories that my colleagues hid from me until the actual interviews so it wouldn't spoil the surprise, which is also why there is so much unprofessional giggling this hour. And full disclosure, I came into this topic a coincidence skeptic, believing that a coincidence is just a coincidence-- nothing more. I judged, I confronted weakness, I countered wide-eyed faith with statistical probability. And by the end, well, stay with us.
<v Sarah Koenig>冒着老土的风险，我还是要说。今天的节目——无巧不成书。趁大老板不在，我们要悄悄把这些巧合故事播出。你们今天听到的所有故事都曾在我们2013年的节目中播出过。它们都是经过核实的真实故事，同事们一直对我保密，直到采访现场才揭晓，以免破坏惊喜，这也是为什么这一小时里有那么多不专业的咯咯笑。坦白说，我在处理这个话题时是一个巧合怀疑论者，认为巧合就是巧合——仅此而已。我曾评判过，对抗过软弱，用统计概率对抗过那些睁大眼睛的信仰。但到了最后，嗯，留下来听听吧。

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:41.300
<v Sarah Koenig>Act One, Grandmas. Blake Oliver is living in Nicaragua right now. We talked on Skype. About four months ago, he was on the phone to his friend Camille, who goes by Cami. He mentioned to her that the screen saver on his iPhone had been the same picture for a long time-- that he wanted to change it.
<v Sarah Koenig>第一幕，祖母们。布雷克·奥利弗现在住在尼加拉瓜。我们通过Skype聊了聊。大约四个月前，他在电话里和朋友卡米尔（大家都叫她卡米）聊天。他提到他iPhone上的屏保一直是同一张照片，很久没变了——他想换掉它。

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:51.920
<v Blake Oliver>So I asked her to just send me a picture of something. And it was really funny. She ended up sending me this picture of her as a little kid as a joke.
<v Blake Oliver>所以我让她随便发一张照片给我。这很有趣。她最终开玩笑地发了一张她小时候的照片给我。

00:09:51.920 --> 00:10:04.530
<v Blake Oliver>And I was like, oh, yeah. That's kind of funny. It was not what I was expecting, but that's funny. And then I glanced back at the picture and I saw my grandma walking through behind her.
<v Blake Oliver>我说，哦，是的。这挺有趣的。不是我预期的，但这很有趣。然后我又瞥了一眼那张照片，看到我的祖母在她身后经过。

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:05.910
<v Sarah Koenig>What?
<v Sarah Koenig>什么？

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:08.430
<v Blake Oliver>Yeah. How crazy is that, right?
<v Blake Oliver>是的。多疯狂啊，对吧？

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:13.110
<v Sarah Koenig>Very crazy. The picture was taken about 18 years ago.
<v Sarah Koenig>非常疯狂。那张照片是大约18年前拍的。

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:15.320
<v Sarah Koenig>But you recognized her right away.
<v Sarah Koenig>但你立刻认出了她。

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:20.520
<v Blake Oliver>Oh, absolutely. There was no question that was Grandma Joyce. It was, without question, Grandma Joyce.
<v Blake Oliver>哦，当然。毫无疑问那是乔伊斯祖母。毫无疑问，那就是乔伊斯祖母。

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:31.920
<v Sarah Koenig>Coincidences like this one usually have a dense nest of details to bolster the craziness. Here goes. Blake is from Michigan. That's where he met Camille. But Camille's family isn't from Michigan at all.
<v Sarah Koenig>像这样的巧合通常有一堆密集的细节来支撑其疯狂程度。听好了。布雷克来自密歇根州。他就是在那里遇见卡米尔的。但卡米尔的家人根本不是密歇根人。

00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:35.190
<v Blake Oliver>They're originally from Utah-- Salt Lake City, Utah.
<v Blake Oliver>他们最初来自犹他州——犹他州盐湖城。

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.410
<v Sarah Koenig>That's where she grew up.
<v Sarah Koenig>那是她长大的地方。

00:10:36.410 --> 00:10:38.100
<v Blake Oliver>Yeah. That's where she grew up.
<v Blake Oliver>是的，那是她长大的地方。

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:41.100
<v Sarah Koenig>Was this photo from Utah?
<v Sarah Koenig>这张照片是在犹他州拍的吗？

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:47.700
<v Blake Oliver>No. And that's-- the funny thing is my grandma lives in Melbourne, Florida. And I'm thinking, oh, she must have been in Utah and walked past it.
<v Blake Oliver>不。有趣的是，我的祖母住在佛罗里达州的墨尔本。我当时想，哦，她肯定当时在犹他州，然后从那儿经过。

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:54.660
<v Blake Oliver>Cami's like, no, no. That picture was taken in Parksville, Vancouver Island, in Canada.
<v Blake Oliver>卡米说，不，不。那张照片是在加拿大的温哥华岛帕克斯维尔拍的。

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:56.190
<v Sarah Koenig>What?
<v Sarah Koenig>什么？

00:10:56.190 --> 00:11:05.670
<v Blake Oliver>Yeah, right?
<v Blake Oliver>是的，对吧？

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:14.040
<v Sarah Koenig>Blake figured out that at the same time that Cami and her family were on vacation in Vancouver, Grandma Joyce had gone there to visit some of her husband's relatives.
<v Sarah Koenig>布雷克推测，就在卡米和她的家人在温哥华度假的同时，乔伊斯祖母也去那里探望她丈夫的一些亲戚。

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:22.740
<v Blake Oliver>What are the chances to think that not only did I meet Cami, but she sends me this picture of her in the background-- of my grandma?
<v Blake Oliver>谁能想到，不仅我遇到了卡米，而且她发给我的这张照片背景里居然有我的祖母？

00:11:22.740 --> 00:11:27.070
<v Sarah Koenig>I don't know what the chances are. Minuscule.
<v Sarah Koenig>我不知道概率是多少。微乎其微。

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:27.530
<v Blake Oliver>Right?
<v Blake Oliver>对吧？

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:31.830
<v Sarah Koenig>Yes!
<v Sarah Koenig>是的！

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:39.010
<v Blake Oliver>And not only-- I don't know if you've seen the picture. But not only just in the picture, but perfectly behind her.
<v Blake Oliver>而且不仅——不知道你有没有看过那张照片。她不仅在照片里，而且正好就在她身后。

00:11:39.010 --> 00:11:51.470
<v Sarah Koenig>At that moment, a fellow producer emailed me the photo. And there it was-- cute, four-year-old Cami in the foreground, facing the camera. And not far behind her, a blonde woman walking past wearing blue shorts and a matching top.
<v Sarah Koenig>就在那一刻，一位同事给我发来了这张照片。照片就在那里——前景是可爱的四岁卡米，正对着镜头。在她身后不远处，一个金发女人穿着蓝色短裤和配套上衣走过。

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:54.250
<v Blake Oliver>Yeah. And she has no idea that she's in the picture even.
<v Blake Oliver>是的。她甚至完全不知道自己出现在照片里。

00:11:54.250 --> 00:12:05.380
<v Sarah Koenig>Yeah, right. It's not like-- yeah. She doesn't seem aware. But I have to say, it's a little-- it's not a very crisp photo. I'm surprised you would recognize her right away.
<v Sarah Koenig>是的，没错。她看起来并不知情。但我不得不说，这照片不是很清晰。我很惊讶你能立刻认出她。

00:12:05.380 --> 00:12:09.340
<v Blake Oliver>Well, yeah. There's not a lot of-- it's my grandma. I don't know. It's like--
<v Blake Oliver>嗯，是的。虽然没有很多细节——但那是我的祖母。我不知道。就好像——

00:12:09.340 --> 00:12:21.690
<v Blake Oliver>[SARAH LAUGHS]
<v Blake Oliver>[莎拉大笑]

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:31.950
<v Sarah Koenig>20 years ago, for my first reporting job, I interviewed a woman who'd just turned 100 years old. I asked her, admittedly, a rookie question-- what's the most amazing thing that's happened in your life?
<v Sarah Koenig>20年前，在我第一份新闻工作中，我采访了一位刚满100岁的老妇人。我问了一个公认的菜鸟问题——你一生中发生过最神奇的事情是什么？

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:51.840
<v Sarah Koenig>She thought for a while and then she said, the most interesting thing that happened to me was that on my first day at Patrog School, the principal said to me, what's your name? I said, Esther Tuttle. And he said, I have a friend in Shelter Island by that name. I was 12, she said. And that, to me, was remarkable.
<v Sarah Koenig>她想了一会儿，然后说，我一生中最有趣的事情是，我在帕特罗格学校的第一天，校长问我，你叫什么名字？我说，艾丝特·塔特尔。他说，我在谢尔特岛有个朋友也叫这个名字。她说，那时我12岁。对我来说，那非常了不起。

00:12:51.840 --> 00:13:14.940
<v Sarah Koenig>This woman had seen the advent of cars and movies and computers and space travel, and this was her answer. At the time, I thought it was lame-- that maybe Esther was losing it a little. But since I started hearing all these coincidence stories the last couple of weeks, I've reconsidered. Coincidences feels so rare, and that sticks with you, even when the coincidence is barely a coincidence at all.
<v Sarah Koenig>这位老妇人见证了汽车、电影、计算机和太空旅行的出现，这就是她的答案。当时，我觉得这很无聊——也许艾丝特有点神志不清了。但自从我过去几周开始听到所有这些巧合故事后，我重新考虑了。巧合感觉太稀少了，所以它会一直留在你脑海里，即使那个巧合勉强算不上巧合。

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:30.870
<v Sarah Koenig>An academic study of people's reactions to coincidence stories from the early 1980s found that we have an egocentric bias toward our own coincidences. That people find stories that happened to them far more surprising than the same stories happening to other people. Case in point.
<v Sarah Koenig>20世纪80年代早期一项关于人们对巧合故事反应的学术研究发现，我们对自己遇到的巧合有一种自我中心偏见。人们认为发生在自己身上的故事远比同样发生在别人身上的故事令人惊讶得多。举个例子。

00:13:30.870 --> 00:13:33.830
<v Juliana Bontrager>My name is Juliana Bontrager and I'm 16 years old.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我叫朱莉安娜·邦特雷格，今年16岁。

00:13:33.830 --> 00:13:36.960
<v Sarah Koenig>Juliana-- she's got a problem with her grandmother.
<v Sarah Koenig>朱莉安娜——她对她的祖母有些看法。

00:13:36.960 --> 00:14:00.090
<v Juliana Bontrager>My grandma has been obsessed with coincidences. And she finds them in her life all the time and she always wants to tell everyone about them. And we get annoyed with her sometimes because she thinks they're so incredible and how they happen to her all the time. And every time we see her-- oh, my gosh. You would not believe what happened to me.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我祖母一直沉迷于巧合。她总是在生活中发现巧合，而且总是想告诉每个人。我们有时会觉得她很烦，因为她觉得这些事情太不可思议了，而且它们一直在她身上发生。每次我们见到她——噢，天哪。你绝对不敢相信刚才发生了什么。

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:22.080
<v Juliana Bontrager>And I always-- I was at the hospital with-- she lives with her cousin so she takes her cousin to the doctor's office quite a bit for appointments and stuff. And she'll be sitting in the waiting room. And then they'll drive to go get lunch after that and they'll see the people they were waiting in the waiting room with at the restaurant.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我总是——我和她一起在医院——她和她的表亲住在一起，所以她经常带她的表亲去医生办公室看病之类的事情。她会坐在候诊室里。后来他们开车去吃午饭，在餐馆里又看见了在候诊室一起等候的人。

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:30.390
<v Juliana Bontrager>And I always thought that was funny. Because it's like, yeah, that's coincidence. But maybe they're just going to get lunch, too. It's not that big of a deal.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我一直觉得这很好笑。因为，嗯，这就是巧合。但也许他们也只是去吃午饭。这没什么大不了的。

00:14:30.390 --> 00:14:30.850
<v Sarah Koenig>Yeah.
<v Sarah Koenig>是的。

00:14:30.850 --> 00:14:51.480
<v Juliana Bontrager>So then, a while ago, I was doing a project in my English class. We were reading The Scarlet Letter book. And I was-- once we finished the book, our end project was we had to come up with what our sin was or what sin we struggled with the most. And I chose envy.
<v Juliana Bontrager>所以，不久前，我在做英语课的课题。我们正在读《红字》这本书。读完书后，我们要做的期末项目是，找出我们的罪恶是什么，或者我们挣扎最多的罪恶是什么。我选择了嫉妒。

00:14:51.480 --> 00:15:08.350
<v Juliana Bontrager>So we had to create our own letter out of paper and then we had to safety pin it to our shirt and wear it around school the entire day. So as I was coloring in my letter and cutting it out, my mom was listening to the show Monk in the background.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我们要用纸剪出自己的字母，然后用别针别在衬衫上，在学校戴上一整天。就在我给字母上色并把它剪下来的时候，我妈妈正在后台听《神探阿蒙》这部剧。

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:30.000
<v Juliana Bontrager>As we were watching it, I was literally cutting out my last line on the letter E for envy. And it said, on the show, I've studied the seven sins and the most prominent one is envy, as I was cutting out my letter. And so I immediately called my grandma and was telling her about it.
<v Juliana Bontrager>当我们在看剧时，我真的正在剪字母E（嫉妒，Envy）的最后一条线。剧里说，我研究了七宗罪，最突出的一宗是嫉妒，正好就在我剪我的字母时。于是我立刻打电话给祖母告诉她这件事。

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:39.960
<v Juliana Bontrager>And she was like, oh, uh-huh. That's a good one. Yeah. And then I didn't really hear back from her after that. And I thought, well, maybe she's just envious of my great coincidence.
<v Juliana Bontrager>她说，哦，嗯哼。那真不错。是的。然后之后就没怎么收到她的回音。我想，好吧，也许她只是嫉妒我这个伟大的巧合。

00:15:39.960 --> 00:15:41.850
<v Juliana Bontrager>[SARAH LAUGHS]
<v Juliana Bontrager>[莎拉大笑]

00:15:41.850 --> 00:15:59.890
<v Juliana Bontrager>And I was sitting there like, are you kidding? Mine was so much better. You just happened to run into a person twice in one day. I do that all the time. What are the odds of, as I'm cutting out this letter E, they would talk about that on a show? And I was so confused about it.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我坐在那里心想，你在开玩笑吗？我的比你的好多了。你只是碰巧一天内遇到同一个人两次。我经常这样做。我在剪这个字母E的时候，他们正好在节目里谈论这个，概率有多大？我对此感到非常困惑。

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:18.870
<v Sarah Koenig>OK. So Juliana's coincidence is a common one. We've all had those moments when you're reading a certain word and then someone says it on the radio or on TV. But remember, Juliana's 16, so maybe this is the first time it's happening to her. Still, I'm on her side here. I think it is more remarkable than her grandmother's example of the people in the waiting room being at the restaurant.
<v Sarah Koenig>好吧。所以朱莉安娜的巧合很常见。我们都曾有过那种正在读某个词，然后电台或电视里就正好说出那个词的时刻。但请记住，朱莉安娜才16岁，所以这可能是她第一次遇到这种事。尽管如此，我站在她这一边。我认为这确实比她祖母提到的在餐馆遇到候诊室里的人的例子更了不起。

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:23.140
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you have a phone where you can dial up your grandma right now?
<v Sarah Koenig>你现在能用手机给你祖母打个电话吗？

00:16:23.140 --> 00:16:24.190
<v Juliana Bontrager>I can try.
<v Juliana Bontrager>我可以试试。

00:16:24.190 --> 00:16:25.720
<v Sarah Koenig>Try it.
<v Sarah Koenig>试试吧。

00:16:25.720 --> 00:16:27.010
<v Juliana Bontrager>OK. One second.
<v Juliana Bontrager>好的，等一下。

00:16:27.010 --> 00:16:30.040
<v Sarah Koenig>So Juliana conferenced her grandmother in so we could settle this.
<v Sarah Koenig>于是朱莉安娜通过电话连线了她的祖母，这样我们就能解决这个争论。

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:32.650
<v Elaine Olson>I'm here. Elaine Olson.
<v Elaine Olson>我在。我是伊莱恩·奥尔森。

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:35.640
<v Sarah Koenig>Elaine Olson. Nice to meet you.
<v Sarah Koenig>伊莱恩·奥尔森。很高兴见到你。

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.280
<v Elaine Olson>Nice to meet you, dear.
<v Elaine Olson>很高兴见到你，亲爱的。

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:50.250
<v Sarah Koenig>I laid out my argument for Elaine about the purity-- and therefore, superiority-- of Juliana's example. But Elaine argued right back. There were several hours between the two sitings, plus, the restaurant was about four miles from the hospital.
<v Sarah Koenig>我向伊莱恩陈述了我的论点，关于朱莉安娜例子的纯粹性——因此是优越性。但伊莱恩立刻反驳了回来。两次相遇之间隔了好几个小时，而且餐馆离医院大约有四英里。

00:16:50.250 --> 00:17:02.010
<v Elaine Olson>And in that four mile radius, you might say, I could have gone to any number of restaurants. The fact that I ran into her at that restaurant, I think is a coincidence.
<v Elaine Olson>在那四英里的范围内，你可能会说，我可以去很多餐厅。我认为我在那家餐厅碰到她是一个巧合。

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:02.390
<v Sarah Koenig>I think--
<v Sarah Koenig>我想——

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.490
<v Elaine Olson>She could have gone any place, too.
<v Elaine Olson>她也可以去任何地方。

00:17:04.490 --> 00:17:16.830
<v Sarah Koenig>That's true. It's true. I'm not saying it's not a coincidence, I'm just saying it's not maybe as pure as-- you can break down the probabilities of a certain kind of person in a certain kind of neighborhood going--
<v Sarah Koenig>那是真的。确实。我不是说这不是巧合，我只是说它可能没有那么纯粹——你可以分解概率，比如某种人在某种社区去某个地方——

00:17:16.830 --> 00:17:17.470
<v Elaine Olson>I don't think so.
<v Elaine Olson>我不这么认为。

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:18.470
<v Sarah Koenig>You don't think so.
<v Sarah Koenig>你不这么认为。

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:21.050
<v Elaine Olson>I don't think so. I think that is--
<v Elaine Olson>我不这么认为。我认为那是——

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:27.440
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you think you're somehow prone to coincidences-- like coincidences find you more than they find other people?
<v Sarah Koenig>你觉得你是否特别容易遇到巧合——就像巧合比其他人更容易找到你一样？

00:17:27.440 --> 00:17:37.280
<v Elaine Olson>I don't know. That's why-- in my own family-- I've asked them, does this happen to you? And don't get much of a response. But--
<v Elaine Olson>我不知道。这就是为什么——在我自己的家庭里——我问过他们，这会发生在你们身上吗？没有得到什么回应。但是——

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:48.140
<v Juliana Bontrager>You are impossible to argue with. So when you ask for coincidences, we have nothing to give to you because you have-- you are so set in your ways.
<v Juliana Bontrager>你真是让人无法辩驳。所以当我们征集巧合时，我们什么也给不了你，因为你——你太固执己见。

00:17:48.140 --> 00:17:50.960
<v Elaine Olson>I'm 81. OK. Yeah. OK.
<v Elaine Olson>我81岁了。好吧。是的。好吧。

00:17:50.960 --> 00:18:00.980
<v Sarah Koenig>So what would Juliana have to experience, in terms of a coincidence, that would impress you, do you think? What would it need to be?
<v Sarah Koenig>那么，朱莉安娜经历什么样的巧合才能打动你呢？你认为需要是什么样的？

00:18:00.980 --> 00:18:12.380
<v Elaine Olson>Well, it's hard to tell. Just a couple of weeks ago, I had my cousin up at another doctor's. I'm sitting in the reception area. Gentlemen across from me was Asian and--
<v Elaine Olson>嗯，很难说。就在几周前，我带着我的表亲去另一位医生那里。我坐在接待区。对面的一位绅士是亚洲人，而且——

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:29.560
<v Sarah Koenig>This time it was about an Asian man in the waiting room whom she later saw buying a melon at Trader Joe's, which Elaine admitted wasn't as good as the other one.
<v Sarah Koenig>这次是关于她在候诊室遇到的一位亚洲男士，后来她在Trader Joe's超市买瓜时又看到了他，伊莱恩承认这确实不如上一个巧合好。

00:18:29.560 --> 00:18:45.440
<v Sarah Koenig>Act Two, In God We Trust. It's probably no surprise that we got a huge number of coincidence pitches about love-- romantic love. A few years ago, after Stephen Lee proposed to his girlfriend Helen, they brought their families together for the first time to celebrate the engagement.
<v Sarah Koenig>第二幕，我们信仰上帝。我们收到大量关于爱情——浪漫爱情的巧合投稿，这可能并不奇怪。几年前，在斯蒂芬·李向女友海伦求婚后，他们第一次把双方家庭聚在一起庆祝订婚。

00:18:45.440 --> 00:19:09.030
<v Stephen Lee>My mom and my stepdad came to New York to meet with Helen's parents. And basically, over the course of dinner and coffee afterwards, we discovered that, basically, my father had dated my wife's mother back in Korea back in the 1960s and he had proposed to her--
<v Stephen Lee>我妈妈和继父来到纽约见海伦的父母。基本上，在晚餐和随后的咖啡时间里，我们发现，我父亲竟然在20世纪60年代在韩国和妻子的母亲约会过，而且他向她求婚了——

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:25.610
<v Sarah Koenig>I'm going to slow this down a sec, just to let it sink in. Helen's mother had almost married Stephen's father-- his late father, actually. He died when Stephen was 17. And how this all came out was that after they had dinner, they went back to Stephen's apartment and they were looking at Stephen's family photographs.
<v Sarah Koenig>我慢一点，让大家消化一下这个信息。海伦的母亲差点就嫁给了斯蒂芬的父亲——实际上是他已故的父亲。他在斯蒂芬17岁时去世了。事情的起因是，他们吃完晚饭回到斯蒂芬的公寓，正在看斯蒂芬的家庭相册。

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:44.460
<v Stephen Lee>So my future mother-in-law's flipping through the album and she sees my dad. And so she asks, oh, oh, what was his name? And my mom tells the name. And my future mother-in-law just nods and moves on and keeps on flipping through the book-- doesn't even say anything.
<v Stephen Lee>我未来的岳母翻着相册看到了我爸爸。她问，哦，哦，他叫什么名字？我妈说了名字。我未来的岳母只是点了点头，继续翻书——什么也没说。

00:19:44.460 --> 00:19:49.640
<v Sarah Koenig>Imagine what she must've been feeling with seeing that photo suddenly-- just flooded.
<v Sarah Koenig>想象一下，她突然看到那张照片时是什么心情——瞬间被淹没了。

00:19:49.640 --> 00:19:49.980
<v Stephen Lee>Yeah.
<v Stephen Lee>是啊。

00:19:49.980 --> 00:19:50.660
<v Sarah Koenig>Crazy.
<v Sarah Koenig>疯狂。

00:19:50.660 --> 00:19:54.870
<v Stephen Lee>She has a-- she just must-- I think she'd be really good at poker.
<v Stephen Lee>她一定——我想她一定非常擅长玩扑克。

00:19:54.870 --> 00:20:01.120
<v Sarah Koenig>(LAUGHING) Right.
<v Sarah Koenig>（大笑）没错。

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:25.430
<v Sarah Koenig>So Helen's mother says nothing, goes home. But later that night, she tells her daughter, this was the one. This was the man who might have been. She explained that the reason they hadn't married was because her father-- Helen's grandfather-- had chosen a different husband for her-- the man who became Helen's father. All of them ended up living in the US, but they quickly lost touch. And Stephen didn't find out about any of this until a couple of days later.
<v Sarah Koenig>海伦的母亲什么也没说，回家了。但那天晚些时候，她告诉女儿，这就是那个男人。这就是那个本可能成为我丈夫的人。她解释说，他们没有结婚的原因是她的父亲——海伦的外祖父——为她选了另一个丈夫——也就是后来成为海伦父亲的那个男人。他们最终都住在美国，但很快就失去了联系。斯蒂芬直到几天后才知道这一切。

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:43.290
<v Stephen Lee>That's when Helen calls me up and says, I have something to tell you. And she tells me and I was floored by it. And together, we called my mom. And she put a good face on it, but she was pretty shocked.
<v Stephen Lee>那时海伦给我打电话说，我有件事要告诉你。她告诉我，我彻底惊呆了。我们一起给妈妈打了电话。她表现得很大度，但她其实非常震惊。

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:46.680
<v Sarah Koenig>Did she know that there had been this other woman that your father had been in love with?
<v Sarah Koenig>她知道你父亲曾经深爱过另一个女人吗？

00:20:46.680 --> 00:21:01.920
<v Stephen Lee>That's the thing. My dad told her, years and years ago, there was this other woman that I was in love with at one point, long before I met you. But Mom was like, look, you have a past. I have a past. We all have a past. Who cares?
<v Stephen Lee>这就是问题所在。我爸爸多年前告诉过她，在他遇见你很久之前，我曾深爱过另一个女人。但我妈说，听着，你有过去。我有过去。我们都有过去。谁在乎呢？

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:14.160
<v Stephen Lee>Of course, never thinking that she was going to share grandchildren with that woman. She later admitted to me that if she had known this from the very beginning, she would have not supported Helen and me.
<v Stephen Lee>当然，她从没想过她会和那个女人共享外孙。她后来向我承认，如果她从一开始就知道这件事，她就不会支持我和海伦在一起了。

00:21:14.160 --> 00:21:15.150
<v Sarah Koenig>Really?
<v Sarah Koenig>真的吗？

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:29.040
<v Stephen Lee>It would have just been like, look, being with the daughter of the other woman your dad loved-- it would-- can't you find someone else?
<v Stephen Lee>那会像是，听着，和你爸爸深爱过的女人的女儿在一起——这会——你就不能找别人吗？

00:21:29.040 --> 00:21:36.870
<v Sarah Koenig>How did you take this coincidence? Did it come to mean anything to you or to Helen?
<v Sarah Koenig>你是怎么看待这个巧合的？这对你或海伦有什么意义吗？

00:21:36.870 --> 00:21:42.370
<v Stephen Lee>Yeah. There's something about it-- the fact that I didn't have the time with my dad that I wished I had had.
<v Stephen Lee>是的。这其中有些东西——我没有和我爸爸相处足够多时间，这是我的遗憾。

00:21:42.370 --> 00:21:43.110
<v Sarah Koenig>Yeah.
<v Sarah Koenig>是啊。

00:21:43.110 --> 00:22:16.480
<v Stephen Lee>And then, suddenly, to have him be an active part of my life again. To think that I can talk to my mother-in-law and hear what he was like in his 20s-- something that my mom doesn't even know. And my-- actually, Helen's father is a strong believer in the idea that somehow my dad-- somehow is behind all this. That somehow he's helped make all this happen.
<v Stephen Lee>然后，突然之间，他再次成为我生活中积极的一部分。想到我可以和我岳母交谈，听听他20多岁时是什么样子——这是我妈妈都不知道的事情。事实上，海伦的父亲坚信，不知何故我爸爸在背后推动了这一切。不知何故是他促成了这一切的发生。

00:22:16.480 --> 00:22:27.450
<v Sarah Koenig>At the time this next love story takes place, a guy named Paul Grachan was working in a Chicago suburb called Arlington Heights. He had just started dating a woman named Esther. They'd been out maybe eight times.
<v Sarah Koenig>在这个接下来的爱情故事发生时，一个叫保罗·格拉坎的家伙在芝加哥郊区阿灵顿高地工作。他刚开始和一个叫埃丝特的女人约会。他们大约约会了八次。

00:22:27.450 --> 00:22:45.960
<v Paul Grachan>And I thought maybe it was time to get exclusive or become boyfriend/girlfriend or whatever it was. And I was about to go out to lunch and I was thinking about asking her on a Friday date that I had set up. And I went out to lunch. I stopped at the store prior to hitting the deli that I normally hit.
<v Paul Grachan>我想也许是时候确立排他性关系，成为男女朋友什么的了。我正要出去吃午饭，在考虑周五向她约会。我去吃午饭。去我常去的那家熟食店之前，我先在店里停了一下。

00:22:45.960 --> 00:23:03.300
<v Paul Grachan>And I got some change, went to the deli, ordered my sandwich. It was $6 or something like that. I pulled out a $5 from my wallet and I pulled out the couple bucks' change that I had gotten from the store, handed the clerk one of the ones. And then I looked at it and I pulled it back.
<v Paul Grachan>我换了一些零钱，去了熟食店，点了三明治。大约6美元。我从钱包里掏出一张5美元的钞票，又掏出在店里换的那几美元零钱，递给店员一张一美元。然后我看了一眼，又把它抽了回来。

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:15.770
<v Paul Grachan>Written on the dollar, in pencil, was Esther. And I thought, oh, that's weird. I'm thinking about asking her to go steady and I get this dollar that says Esther on it. That's bizarre. So I stuck it in my pocket, chuckled.
<v Paul Grachan>钞票上用铅笔写着“埃丝特”。我想，哦，这太奇怪了。我正想着约她交往，就收到了这张写着埃丝特名字的钞票。这太离奇了。所以我把它揣进兜里，笑了笑。

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:40.640
<v Paul Grachan>I went to the store. I got one of those floating frames and a little piece of gold lame and I stuck the dollar in the frame. So it was floating in the middle on this piece of gold, as if you're going to go in a church in Italy and they had a fragment from the robe of some saint or something like that. And I called it the Immaculate Dollar of Arlington Heights.
<v Paul Grachan>我去了商店。我买了那种悬浮相框和一点金丝边，把钞票嵌在里面。它浮在金丝中央，就像你要去意大利的一座教堂，那里有某位圣人的长袍碎片之类的东西一样。我把它称为“阿灵顿高地的无暇之美元”。

00:23:40.640 --> 00:23:51.560
<v Paul Grachan>So on Friday, I asked her out and she was all excited and we were all giddy. And then I said, hey, I have one more thing for you. And I gave her this frame with this dollar in it.
<v Paul Grachan>周五，我约她出来，她很兴奋，我们也都很激动。然后我说，嘿，我还有样东西给你。我把那个带相框的美元给了她。

00:23:51.560 --> 00:24:03.310
<v Esther Grachan>I start unwrapping it and he's like, it's a dollar with your name on it. And he's all beside himself. And I just looked at him like, I'm freaked out.
<v Esther Grachan>我开始拆包装，他说，这是一张写着你名字的美元。他自己都惊呆了。我看着他，心想，我吓坏了。

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:12.580
<v Paul Grachan>She furrowed her brow a little bit and look concerned. And I said, is there some sort of problem?
<v Paul Grachan>她微微皱了皱眉，看起来很担心。我说，是有什么问题吗？

00:24:12.580 --> 00:24:23.620
<v Esther Grachan>I was speechless for the first time ever. But I said, you know, remind me to tell you something later.
<v Esther Grachan>我有生以来第一次说不出话来。但我说，你知道，以后提醒我告诉你一件事。

00:24:23.620 --> 00:24:45.220
<v Paul Grachan>So OK, I let it go and we had our relationship. We got engaged. We got married. A couple of years later, we were unpacking at a new apartment that we had. And I had the-- I found the frame with the dollar in it. And I stuck it up on the dresser and she came in the room. I said, hey, you never told me about this dollar. What's the deal with that? She said, well--
<v Paul Grachan>好吧，我没再问。我们继续交往。我们订婚了，结婚了。几年后，我们在新公寓里整理行李。我发现了那个嵌着美元的相框。我把它放在梳妆台上。她走进房间，我说，嘿，你从来没告诉过我关于这张美元的事。那是怎么回事？她说，嗯——

00:24:45.220 --> 00:25:03.800
<v Esther Grachan>When I was 19, I was a cashier working in a copy shop in downtown Chicago. And I was dating someone and I just wasn't happy with him. And I just thought, how do people know who the right person that they're meant to be with-- how do they know who that is?
<v Esther Grachan>我19岁时，是芝加哥市中心一家复印店的出纳。当时我在和一个男人约会，但我并不开心。我心想，人们怎么知道谁是他们命中注定的那个人呢——他们怎么知道那个人是谁？

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:12.790
<v Esther Grachan>I said, you know what? I'm not going to worry about that. I'm just going to put my name on this dollar bill. And the guy that gets this dollar bill is going to be the guy that asks me to marry him.
<v Esther Grachan>我说，你知道吗？我不管这些了。我要把我的名字写在这张美元钞票上。拿到这张钞票的那个男人就是我要嫁的人。

00:25:12.790 --> 00:25:33.160
<v Esther Grachan>And I'm like, well, you can't just write your name on one. Actually, I think it was more like maybe 10 or 12. And so this dollar bill that you gave me, I believe, is the same dollar bill that I wrote my name on. And I knew that we were going to be married the day that you gave me this dollar bill.
<v Esther Grachan>我心想，好吧，你不能只写一张。实际上，我想可能是10或12张。所以，我给你的这张美元钞票，我相信，就是我写上名字的那张。我给你这张钞票的那天，我就知道我们要结婚了。

00:25:33.160 --> 00:25:34.480
<v Paul Grachan>It was pretty crazy.
<v Paul Grachan>这简直太疯狂了。

00:25:34.480 --> 00:25:53.200
<v Esther Grachan>And then he made me tell him the story again. He asked me five or six times. And I said, the details don't change. It's the same story. He couldn't-- I think he was a little bit beside himself that that actually happened and that I didn't tell him.
<v Esther Grachan>然后他让我把故事又讲了一遍。他问了五六次。我说，细节没变。故事还是那个故事。他不能——我想他当时自己都惊呆了，这件事真的发生了，而且我没告诉他。

00:25:53.200 --> 00:26:09.770
<v Esther Grachan>I don't know how I found the will to not say anything to him, but I just remember do I, don't I, do I, don't I? What do I do? I didn't want to freak this guy out and be like, what, this girl's already talking about marriage? Wait, what? Forget it.
<v Esther Grachan>我不知道我是怎么忍住没告诉他的，但我只记得在纠结，说还是不说，说还是不说？我该怎么办？我不想吓跑这家伙，如果让他觉得，什么，这女孩已经在谈婚论嫁了？等等，什么？算了吧。

00:26:09.770 --> 00:26:37.380
<v Paul Grachan>I don't read too much into almost anything. I just kind of move forward. And then after she told me, I think I went through all the normal stages of whatever and I just thought, what does this mean for us? Are we going to invent a time machine or our kids going to bring world peace? What's the-- is there some sort of bigger thing that we're not seeing here?
<v Paul Grachan>我几乎不从这些事里解读出什么。我只是继续前进。她告诉我之后，我想我经历了所有正常的阶段，然后我想，这对我们意味着什么？我们要发明时光机还是我们的孩子会给世界带来和平？有什么更宏大的东西是我们没看到的吗？

00:26:37.380 --> 00:26:50.960
<v Paul Grachan>Now it just sits in that very same frame up on our dresser and I look at it every day that I wake up. Sometimes I remind her that we have it when she's upset at me or angry.
<v Paul Grachan>现在它依然放在梳妆台那个相同的相框里，我每天醒来都会看看它。有时当她生我的气时，我会提醒她我们有这张钞票。

00:26:50.960 --> 00:27:10.150
<v Esther Grachan>It's never been a question in my mind like, you know what? I'm going to kick this guy to the curb. But I do believe it's because of this dollar that I feel tied to him. Obviously, I love him, but I think that if it ever did cross my mind, I'd be like, but wait, this is my soulmate. I can't just walk out on him.
<v Esther Grachan>我从未想过，你知道吗？我要把这家伙踢开。但我确实相信是因为这张美元，我才感觉和他绑在一起。显然我爱他，但我想如果我曾有过离开他的念头，我会想，但等等，这是我的灵魂伴侣。我不能就这么离开他。

00:27:10.150 --> 00:27:31.290
<v Esther Grachan>We don't even wear wedding bands. I'm like, why? I know I'm stuck with you. You got this dollar bill. I guess he could also put it back out into the register and then be like, look, I'm cashing this in. I think it's got to go to somebody else. He could do that.
<v Esther Grachan>我们甚至不戴婚戒。我说，为什么？我知道我被你缠住了。你有这张美元钞票。我想他也可以把它放回收银台，然后说，看，我把它兑现了。我想它该给别人了。他可以这么做。

00:27:31.290 --> 00:27:35.350
<v Sarah Koenig>Paul and Esther Grachan. They've been married for 18 years.
<v Sarah Koenig>保罗和埃丝特·格拉坎。他们结婚18年了。

00:27:35.350 --> 00:28:20.040
<v Sarah Koenig>[MUSIC - "SOMEPLACE I'M SUPPOSED TO BE" BY BRY WEBB WITH RICH BURNETT]
<v Sarah Koenig>[音乐 - "Someplace I'm Supposed to Be" by Bry Webb with Rich Burnett]

00:28:20.040 --> 00:28:26.690
<v Sarah Koenig>Act Three, Brother Can You Spare a Dime? This story comes from Ryan Rozar in Los Angeles.
<v Sarah Koenig>第三幕，兄弟，能给点零钱吗？这个故事来自洛杉矶的瑞安·罗扎尔。

00:28:26.690 --> 00:29:05.630
<v Ryan Rozar>It happened when I was in college. I was actually pledging a fraternity. And I ran into some of the seniors on campus and they were laughing about something. And so one of them tells me this story that had happened to him that day, supposedly, where he gets in the shower and about 5, 10 minutes into his shower, he hears a "ting." And he looks down and sitting on the floor of the shower between his feet is a nickel. And has no idea where this nickel came from or why it's there.
<v Ryan Rozar>发生在我上大学的时候。我当时正在预备加入一个兄弟会。我在校园里遇到几个大四学生，他们正在笑什么。其中一个人告诉了我他那天所谓发生的经历，他洗澡洗了5、10分钟，听到“叮”的一声。他低头一看，两脚之间的地板上躺着一枚镍币。他完全不知道这镍币是从哪儿来的，为什么会在那儿。

00:29:05.630 --> 00:29:36.330
<v Ryan Rozar>Obviously, everyone was making jokes that he had change falling out of his butt and we were like, were you eating dollar bills? But anyway, we have our laughs and I go home and I don't think much about it. The next day, I get in the shower and like 5, 10 minutes into my shower, I hear a "ting." And I look down and right between my feet is a quarter.
<v Ryan Rozar>显然，大家都在开玩笑说他屁股里掉了零钱出来，我们问，你是不是在吃一美元钞票？总之，我们笑过了，我也回家了，没多想。第二天，我洗澡，5、10分钟后，听到“叮”的一声。我低头一看，两脚之间正好是一枚25美分硬币。

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:54.350
<v Ryan Rozar>So my first thought was, how did they do it? My thought was, I totally assumed that this must not have really happened to him. That was a set up and somehow they set this up to happen to me. But I could not figure out how. I was just like, it had to be a prank, basically.
<v Ryan Rozar>所以我的第一个想法是，他们是怎么做到的？我觉得这肯定不是真的发生在他身上。那是设计好的圈套，不知何故他们设置了这一切来整我。但我算不出怎么做到的。我只是觉得，这基本上是个恶作剧。

00:29:54.350 --> 00:30:00.920
<v Sarah Koenig>Oh, that's so smart of you to figure out the whole con.
<v Sarah Koenig>哦，你真聪明，识破了整个骗局。

00:30:00.920 --> 00:30:01.850
<v Ryan Rozar>That's what I assumed.
<v Ryan Rozar>我当时就是这么想的。

00:30:01.850 --> 00:30:02.150
<v Sarah Koenig>Right.
<v Sarah Koenig>对。

00:30:02.150 --> 00:30:11.580
<v Ryan Rozar>But then I go and I talk to him I tell him what happened to me. And I'm watching his face for that-- the laugh or the glint in his eye of mischief or whatever.
<v Ryan Rozar>但后来我去找他，告诉了他我身上发生的事。我在观察他的表情，看有没有那种笑意或者恶作剧的闪光什么的。

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:11.980
<v Sarah Koenig>Right.
<v Sarah Koenig>对。

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:32.240
<v Ryan Rozar>And he just looks sincerely mystified by the fact that this happened to me right after it happened to him. Then a week later, I get in the shower and like 5, 10 minutes in, I hear "ting, ting." And I look down and there's about $0.35 between my feet. Right?
<v Ryan Rozar>但他看起来真的很困惑，我不久前才发生的这件事，发生在他之后。然后一周后，我洗澡，洗了5、10分钟，听到“叮，叮”。我低头一看，两脚之间有大约35美分的零钱。对吧？

00:30:32.240 --> 00:30:35.140
<v Ryan Rozar>[SARAH LAUGHS]
<v Ryan Rozar>[莎拉大笑]

00:30:35.140 --> 00:31:01.210
<v Ryan Rozar>So there's that moment of no way before I look down and there's more change. So at this point, obviously, my mind's going to some weird places because I-- you don't want to be the butt change fairy guy. There's somehow-- there's just-- there's nothing-- no way that this story gets good for me.
<v Ryan Rozar>所以那一刻我还没来得及低头看就有了那种“不会吧”的想法，而且地上还有更多的零钱。此时此刻，显然我的头脑转向了一些奇怪的地方，因为你——你不想成为那个“屁股掉零钱的家伙”。这中间——没有任何——没有任何理由让这个故事对我有什么好结果。

00:31:01.210 --> 00:31:23.440
<v Ryan Rozar>And then no explanation comes, but it doesn't happen for a while-- for I want to say, probably, a month. Until one day, I'm about to get in the shower and I reach behind me to scratch my back and I feel something cold and metal on my back. And I turn around to show my-- to turn my back to the mirror--
<v Ryan Rozar>然后并没有什么解释出现，但有一段时间——我想可能是一个月——没发生。直到有一天，我正要洗澡，伸手到背后挠痒，感觉背后有些冰凉的金属物。我转身展示我的——背对着镜子——

00:31:23.440 --> 00:31:23.860
<v Sarah Koenig>Right.
<v Sarah Koenig>对。

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:29.480
<v Ryan Rozar>And there is a whole pocketful of change stuck to my back?
<v Ryan Rozar>一整口袋零钱粘在我的背上？

00:31:29.480 --> 00:31:31.840
<v Sarah Koenig>What do you mean stuck to it? In what way?
<v Sarah Koenig>你是什么意思粘在上面？什么方式？

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:56.010
<v Ryan Rozar>Stuck to it. Well, this is what I figured out. It wasn't-- there was no adhesive or anything. It was just impressed into my back. So I figured out what was going on is that I have a bad habit of falling asleep with my pants on because it was college, I would nap a lot. And I would also sleep with my shirt off because my apartment was really hot.
<v Ryan Rozar>粘在上面。这就是我弄清楚的事情。它不是——没有胶水或什么东西。它只是压进了我的背部。所以我明白是怎么回事了，我有习惯穿着裤子睡觉，因为那时是大学，我经常打盹。而且我也经常光着上身睡觉，因为我的公寓很热。

00:31:56.010 --> 00:32:28.370
<v Ryan Rozar>So what was happening was change was falling out of my pockets onto my bed and I was laying on it and all that sweat and pressure was just sticking the coins to my back. They're just literally impressed into my back. And it was taking about five minutes of water running on it to dislodge them, at which point they were dropping at my feet.
<v Ryan Rozar>发生的事情是零钱从我的口袋里掉到了床上，我躺在上面，所有的汗水和压力只是把硬币粘在了我的背上。它们真的就是压进了我的背部。花大约五分钟的水流冲洗，它们就会松动，然后落到我的脚边。

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:39.830
<v Sarah Koenig>Clearly, I love this story, but I did not see the coincidence in it. Ryan said there was absolutely a coincidence. It happened to that one kid-- the senior-- and then it happened to him the very next day.
<v Sarah Koenig>显然，我很喜欢这个故事，但我没看出其中的巧合。瑞安说那绝对是个巧合。它发生在那一个孩子——那个大四学生身上，然后第二天就发生在他身上。

00:32:39.830 --> 00:32:52.400
<v Sarah Koenig>I disagree. I think this is probably a common quirk of college boy life-- lounging around, shirtless and groggy, on unclean sofas and beds. Probably half the boys at his college had change stuck to them. I told Ryan so.
