<v Jeff Dunn>I don't even know the coincidence of my own story. Yeah, I submitted it to the coincidences thing.
<v Jeff Dunn>我甚至不知道我自己的故事里有什么巧合。是的，我把它提交给那个巧合节目了。

00:41:16.010 --> 00:41:19.290
<v Sarah Koenig>So the main coincidence is there's a girl missing at the same time.
<v Sarah Koenig>所以主要的巧合是同时有一个女孩失踪了。

00:41:19.290 --> 00:41:20.180
<v Jeff Dunn>Yeah.
<v Jeff Dunn>是的。

00:41:20.180 --> 00:41:21.710
<v Sarah Koenig>I have five words for you, Jeff.
<v Sarah Koenig>杰夫，我有五个字送给你。

00:41:21.710 --> 00:41:23.630
<v Yuen Yuen Di>[SPEAKING MANDARIN]
<v Yuen Yuen Di>[说普通话]

00:41:23.630 --> 00:41:30.200
<v Sarah Koenig>No coincidence, no story.
<v Sarah Koenig>无巧不成书。

00:41:30.200 --> 00:41:45.670
<v Sarah Koenig>Here's a definition of coincidence that seems right to me. A coincidence is a surprising concurrence of events perceived as meaningfully related with no apparent causal connection. It's that middle part-- meaningfully related-- that people seem to get stuck on.
<v Sarah Koenig>这是一个我觉得对巧合很准确的定义。巧合是一系列令人惊讶的事件并发，被感知为意义相关，但没有任何明显的因果联系。正是中间那一部分——“意义相关”——人们似乎卡住了。

00:41:45.670 --> 00:42:00.280
<v Sarah Koenig>Because when events line up just so, you can't help it. You can't help but wonder if there's a message in that. In that way, coincidences are like shortcuts to very big questions about fate, about God, even to people who don't believe in either one.
<v Sarah Koenig>因为当事件刚好凑在一起时，你无法抗拒。你忍不住想知道这其中是否有什么信息。从这个角度看，巧合就像通往关于命运、上帝等宏大问题的捷径，即使是对那些两者都不信的人来说也是如此。

00:42:00.280 --> 00:42:21.760
<v Sarah Koenig>The notion that somewhere out there, someone or something is paying attention to your life-- that there might be a plan conjured through coincidences-- that notion seems to be most appealing when you're young. We got one story about a young couple visiting Marrakesh in Morocco. They're at a restaurant talking, like you do, about old flames. And the boyfriend wanted to know about one guy in particular.
<v Sarah Koenig>那种认为在某个地方，某人或某物正在关注你的生活——那种认为通过巧合可能存在某种计划——这种观念似乎在年轻人中最为吸引人。我们收到一个关于一对在摩洛哥马拉喀什度假的年轻情侣的故事。他们在餐馆聊天，谈论旧爱，就像你们常做的那样。男朋友想知道关于一个特定男人的事。

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:44.420
<v Erik Johnson>I asked, Kate, Kate, what did this guy look like? And Kate just stopped dead in her tracks and lifted one hand to her mouth, with the other hand pointed across the restaurant. And she said, oh, my god. He looked like that. And she crossed the restaurant and tapped him on the back of the shoulder. And it was him.
<v Erik Johnson>我问，凯特，凯特，那个男人长什么样？凯特整个人僵住了，一只手捂住嘴，另一只手指向餐厅对面。她说，天哪。他看起来就是那样。她穿过餐厅，拍了拍他的肩膀。真的是他。

00:42:44.420 --> 00:42:51.470
<v Sarah Koenig>The daughter of a jazz guitarist named Andy Riley wrote to us about the time when she was 19. She was listening to an Ella Fitzgerald album.
<v Sarah Koenig>一位爵士吉他手安迪·莱利的女儿写信给我们，讲了她19岁时的一件事。她在听埃拉·费茨杰拉德的专辑。

00:42:51.470 --> 00:43:07.600
<v Leila Wright>I was listening to the song, "That Old Black Magic." And at the end of the song, Ella throws in a line, "that old black magic that Billy Daniels got me in." And I've heard her sing that line on that album dozens of times before and wondered, who's Billy Daniels?
<v Leila Wright>我在听那首《That Old Black Magic》。在歌曲结尾，埃拉加入了一句歌词，“that old black magic that Billy Daniels got me in”。我以前在这张专辑里听她唱过无数次这句歌词，心想，比利·丹尼尔斯是谁？

00:43:07.600 --> 00:43:27.260
<v Leila Wright>But on this particular day, for some reason, I felt inspired to go out to the living room and ask my dad who Billy Daniels was. And he explained that in the '40s, Billy Daniels had sung "Old Black Magic" in a way that made it his song. And he was referred to as Mr. Black Magic, I guess, back in the '40s. And it was a big deal at the time.
<v Leila Wright>但在这一天，出于某种原因，我感觉受到启发，走到客厅问我爸比利·丹尼尔斯是谁。他解释说，在40年代，比利·丹尼尔斯唱过《Old Black Magic》，唱出了属于他自己的风格。在40年代，他被称为“黑魔法先生”，我想是这样。在当时那是件大事。

00:43:27.260 --> 00:43:34.930
<v Leila Wright>And I asked my dad, had you met him? Did you ever know Billy Daniels? And he said that Billy Daniels was probably dead now.
<v Leila Wright>我问我爸，你见过他吗？你认识比利·丹尼尔斯吗？他说比利·丹尼尔斯现在大概已经死了。

00:43:34.930 --> 00:43:58.850
<v Leila Wright>And literally at that moment, the phone rang. So I answer the phone. And this playful, gravelly voice asked for Andy Riley. And I said, who's calling? And he said, Billy Daniels.
<v Leila Wright>就在那一刻，电话响了。我接了电话。一个俏皮、沙哑的声音询问安迪·莱利。我说，谁在打？他说，比利·丹尼尔斯。

00:43:58.850 --> 00:44:07.520
<v Etta Thordarson>So, last September, I was living in Roswell, New Mexico. And I had woken up in jail for the last time.
<v Etta Thordarson>去年9月，我住在新墨西哥州的罗斯韦尔。我最后一次在监狱醒来。

00:44:07.520 --> 00:44:18.860
<v Sarah Koenig>This is Etta Thordarson. When this happened, she was 20 years old, on drugs, hanging out with street kids. Her mom offered to buy her a bus ticket to Washington state to go to rehab. Etta said, yes.
<v Sarah Koenig>这是埃塔·索达森。这件事发生时，她才20岁，吸毒，和街头少年混在一起。她妈妈提出给她买一张去华盛顿州接受戒毒治疗的车票。埃塔答应了。

00:44:18.860 --> 00:44:32.090
<v Sarah Koenig>She took her dog and got on the bus. In Los Angeles, there was a stopover at the depot and this guy comes up to her while she's waiting in line to get food. She's got $2-- just enough for some French fries. The guy starts asking her questions about her dog.
<v Sarah Koenig>她带着狗上了车。在洛杉矶车站转车时，她排队等食物时，一个男人向她走来。她身上有2美元——刚好够买点炸薯条。那人开始问她关于狗的问题。

00:44:32.090 --> 00:44:40.970
<v Etta Thordarson>And he starts asking me other random questions, like, what are you doing? Where are you going? And then he asks me, are you from Alaska?
<v Etta Thordarson>他开始问我其他一些随机问题，比如，你在做什么？你要去哪儿？然后他问我，你是阿拉斯加人吗？

00:44:40.970 --> 00:44:51.150
<v Etta Thordarson>And I was like, yeah, I am, actually. And then he asked me, is your mom named Meg? And I was like, mm-hmm. Yeah. She is.
<v Etta Thordarson>我说，是的，确实。然后他问我，你妈妈叫梅格吗？我说，嗯嗯。是的。她是。

00:44:51.150 --> 00:44:58.610
<v Etta Thordarson>And he was like, oh, well-- well, I'm Chris. I'm your father. I met you 10 years ago. And I was like, oh, holy [BEEP].
<v Etta Thordarson>他说，哦，嗯——我是克里斯。我是你父亲。我10年前见过你。我说，噢，该死的[哔]。

00:44:58.610 --> 00:45:02.860
<v Sarah Koenig>Etta had only seen her father that one time, briefly, when she was 11.
<v Sarah Koenig>埃塔只在那次见过她父亲，那是她11岁时短暂的一次。

00:45:02.860 --> 00:45:17.200
<v Etta Thordarson>And then he pulls out a big wad of money and he pays for my food. And then he hands me $40. And was like, don't say I never gave you nothing and walked away.
<v Etta Thordarson>然后他掏出一大把钱，为我的食物买了单。然后递给我40美元。说，别说我没给过你什么，然后走开了。

00:45:17.200 --> 00:45:25.170
<v Sarah Koenig>That-- what-- I-- I'm shocked by this story. And he didn't-- that was it? You didn't see him again? He didn't--
<v Sarah Koenig>这——什么——我——我被这个故事震惊了。他就——那是全部吗？你没再见过他？他没——

00:45:25.170 --> 00:45:32.750
<v Etta Thordarson>No. You could tell he felt bad.
<v Etta Thordarson>没有。你能看出他觉得不好意思。

00:45:32.750 --> 00:45:37.740
<v Sarah Koenig>Did you expect something more to happen? Did you think, oh, well, here's how this is going to go.
<v Sarah Koenig>你期待发生更多事吗？你觉得，哦，好了，这事要这样发展了。

00:45:37.740 --> 00:46:04.820
<v Etta Thordarson>Yeah. I really thought we were going to go sit down and eat together and I was going to lay out my whole life story for him and he was going to explain his life and where he's been. I was looking forward to that. But it didn't happen. Mostly, I want to study his face for longer just because that's-- I've seen him twice and I still can't really remember what he looks like.
<v Etta Thordarson>是的。我真的以为我们会坐下来一起吃饭，我要对他讲述我整个人生故事，他会解释他的生活以及他去了哪儿。我很期待。但那没发生。大部分时间，我只是想更长时间地观察他的脸，因为——我只见过他两次，我还是记不住他长什么样。

00:46:04.820 --> 00:46:09.980
<v Sarah Koenig>Do you regret not pursuing him in some way?
<v Sarah Koenig>你后悔没有以某种方式去寻找他吗？

00:46:09.980 --> 00:46:42.550
<v Etta Thordarson>Yeah, absolutely. In the moment, I was just-- I was so sick. I just really couldn't comprehend. It wasn't until I got on the bus that I was just-- why didn't I run after him? I've just spent my whole life wondering about this man and the chance to talk to him was put right in front of my face and I didn't really understand that at the time. And I let it go.
<v Etta Thordarson>是的，绝对后悔。那一刻，我只是——我太糟糕了。我真的无法理解。直到我上了车我才——我为什么没追他？我整辈子都在好奇这个男人，谈话的机会就摆在我的面前，当时我没真正意识到。我让他走了。

00:46:42.550 --> 00:46:58.680
<v Sarah Koenig>Emiliana Garcia-Sarnoff also hadn't seen his father in many years, since he was seven or eight years old. He lived in California, but he was visiting a national park in Mexico with his mother when he was 18 and they hiked to the top of a pyramid there. The whole place was sort of off the beaten track.
<v Sarah Koenig>艾米利亚娜·加西亚-萨诺夫也很多年没见过父亲了，从他七八岁开始。他住在加州，但他18岁时和母亲去墨西哥的一家国家公园游玩，他们徒步爬上了那里的金字塔顶端。那个地方基本上远离尘世。

00:46:58.680 --> 00:47:18.090
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>We were walking along and some people passed in front of us and my mom said, oh, my god. Raul? And then she turned to me and said, that's your father. That's your dad. And it was.
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>我们走着走着，有人从我们前面经过，我妈说，噢，天哪。劳尔？然后她转向我说，那是你父亲。那是你爸爸。真的是。

00:47:18.090 --> 00:47:21.150
<v Sarah Koenig>Also, coincidentally, it was Father's Day.
<v Sarah Koenig>而且巧合的是，那天正好是父亲节。

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:33.320
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>I ended up, embarrassingly, breaking down and crying on that pyramid. It was a really emotional moment for me. My father definitely-- his absence meant a lot to me. And we said, yeah, we're going to stay in contact.
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>我最后尴尬地在那个金字塔上崩溃大哭。对我来说那是一个非常感性的时刻。我父亲确实——他的缺席对我意义重大。我们说，是的，我们要保持联系。

00:47:33.320 --> 00:47:54.090
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>And after they said bye, they left. And he came running back up the hill about 20 minutes later. And you could hear him panting. And he said, Emiliano, I just want to say I'm sorry that I didn't stay in touch and that I love you. And he turned and went back down the hill.
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>告别后，他们离开了。大约20分钟后，他跑回了山上。你能听到他气喘吁吁。他说，艾米利亚诺，我只是想说我很抱歉我没保持联系，我爱你。然后他转身走下了山。

00:47:54.090 --> 00:48:18.780
<v Sarah Koenig>Emiliano's story goes the way Etta wishes hers had gone. After a rocky few years, he and his father did get to know each other and each other's families. Emiliano had a son who now had a grandfather. Emiliano's father had left him when he was a baby, but when his father died some years ago, Emiliano was there at the hospital. They'd had a real relationship and that never would have happened, Emiliano said, if he hadn't seen him that day at the pyramid.
<v Sarah Koenig>艾米利亚诺的故事走向了埃塔所希望的那个方向。经过几年的坎坷，他和父亲确实了解了对方，也了解了对方的家庭。艾米利亚诺有了一个儿子，现在也有了祖父。艾米利亚诺的父亲在他婴儿时期就离开了他，但当他父亲几年前去世时，艾米利亚诺在医院。他们建立了一段真正的关系，艾米利亚诺说，如果那天在金字塔没见到他，这一切永远不会发生。

00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:35.130
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>Walking down that pyramid, I was talking out loud to myself. And I was saying-- this is one strong memory I have. I was saying, don't forget, don't forget. Just getting angry with my future self, which I knew would rationalize this event.
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>走下金字塔时，我在自言自语。我说——这是一个我记忆很深的时刻。我说，别忘了，别忘了。只是对自己未来的自我感到愤怒，因为我知道未来会把这件事合理化。

00:48:35.130 --> 00:48:55.000
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>Because I had a very distinct sense that I had felt that this was definitely a miracle. Something had intervened and brought us together. You don't just run into your father on top of a pyramid. So I think I was a believer for about a week. And then--
<v Emiliano Garcia-sarnoff>因为我有一种非常清晰的感觉，我觉得这绝对是一个奇迹。有什么力量干预并让我们聚在一起。你不会就那样在金字塔顶端撞见你父亲。所以我想我大概相信了一周。然后——

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:58.160
<v Sarah Koenig>And then, that very pause--
<v Sarah Koenig>然后，那短暂的暂停——

00:48:58.160 --> 00:49:21.410
<v Sarah Koenig>That brings us to Act Five, What Are the Chances? In order to let a good coincidence live a long and healthy life-- to get told and retold and take its rightful place in the narrative of your life-- sometimes you have to stop yourself from thinking it all the way through-- why it happened, really. Because the fact is, any statistician will probably rain on your parade, will tell you these things happen a lot more often than you think.
<v Sarah Koenig>这把我们带到了第五幕，“概率是多少？”为了让一个好的巧合拥有长久而健康的生命——被讲述、再讲述，并在你的人生叙事中占据应有的位置——有时你必须阻止自己想得太透彻——它为什么发生，真正的原因。因为事实是，任何统计学家都可能会破坏你的兴致，会告诉你这些事情发生的频率比你想象的要多得多。

00:49:21.410 --> 00:49:38.330
<v Sarah Koenig>But a good coincidence is like a good magic trick. When you see one, a struggle ensues instantaneously between the thrill of the apparent miracle and the urge to debunk it. This final story is by Kerry Weeks. He's a prop guy for the movies, but he told this at a storytelling competition.
<v Sarah Koenig>但一个好的巧合就像一个好的魔术。当你看到一个时，那种对看似奇迹的兴奋感和想要揭穿它的冲动之间会立刻爆发一场斗争。这个最后的故事来自凯瑞·威克斯。他是电影道具师，但在一次故事比赛中讲述了这个故事。

00:49:38.330 --> 00:50:06.130
<v Kerry Weeks>Here's my story. I'm nine years old-- first time going to summer camp. And my grandfather gives me-- before I go, he gives me his old pocket knife. It's practically an antique. And so the first thing all of us boys do when we get to camp is we pull out our knives and we start throwing them at things, trying to get them to stick the way Daniel Boone did. And because it's the 1970s, there's nothing unusual about a bunch of young children throwing sharp knives around.
<v Kerry Weeks>这是我的故事。我9岁——第一次去夏令营。在我出发前，祖父给了我一把他的旧折叠刀。它简直是个古董。我们男孩到营地的第一件事就是掏出刀子朝东西扔，试图模仿丹尼尔·布恩把它扎住。因为那是20世纪70年代，一群小孩乱扔尖刀没什么不寻常的。

00:50:06.130 --> 00:50:07.550
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:50:07.550 --> 00:50:17.410
<v Kerry Weeks>And I got mine to stick in the floorboard. It was like, "wong." And it was great. But when I pulled it out, the tip broke off.
<v Kerry Weeks>我把它扎进了地板上。感觉像“嗡”。太棒了。但当我把它拔出来时，刀尖断了。

00:50:17.410 --> 00:50:25.600
<v Kerry Weeks>But anyway, camp goes on. And a few days later, I notice my knife is missing. And I look everywhere for it. I look high, I look low. It's gone.
<v Kerry Weeks>总之，夏令营继续。几天后，我发现刀丢了。我到处找。我找了高处，找了低处。它没了。

00:50:25.600 --> 00:50:52.630
<v Kerry Weeks>But then one day, I'm walking through camp and I see this group of boys throwing knives at a tree and something tells me I should check this out. And sure enough, there's this boy in there throwing my knife. Now, there's no mistaking this knife because the handle was red and green, but the colors met in the middle and kind of swirled together in a trippy, psychedelic way. And I've never seen a knife like that before or since.
<v Kerry Weeks>但有一天，我走在营地里，看到一群男孩在朝树扔刀子，有什么东西告诉我该去看看。果然，有个男孩在扔我的刀。现在，这把刀没法认错，因为刀柄是红绿色的，但在中间汇合，以一种迷幻的、疯疯癫癫的方式旋转着。我从未见过那样的刀。

00:50:52.630 --> 00:51:06.390
<v Kerry Weeks>So I walk up to this boy. He's a little older, but I walk up to him and I say, hey, that's my knife. And he says, no, it's mine. And I say, no, that's my knife. My grandfather gave it to me. And he says, no, he didn't.
<v Kerry Weeks>所以我走向那个男孩。他稍微大一点，但我走过去说，嘿，那是我的刀。他说，不，那是我的。我说，不，那是我的刀。我祖父给我的。他说，没有，他没给。

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:09.180
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:51:09.180 --> 00:51:36.240
<v Kerry Weeks>He says, my grandfather gave it to me. And I'm confused all of a sudden. Maybe I should have thought this through a bit more. I thought he would just hand it over. And the other boys-- they move in close. And I'm thinking, could it be possible that his grandfather gave him the exact same knife that my grandfather gave me? Is this a thing and I just didn't know it?
<v Kerry Weeks>他说，我祖父给我的。我突然困惑了。也许我应该稍微想清楚一点。我以为他会直接交出来的。其他男孩——他们围了过来。我在想，难道他的祖父真的给了他和我祖父给我的完全一样的刀吗？这东西很普遍而我不知道吗？

00:51:36.240 --> 00:51:38.720
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:51:38.720 --> 00:52:01.910
<v Kerry Weeks>But I look at the knife he's holding in his hand and I look at the blade and I see that the tip of the blade is squared off, as though it were broken. And I say, that's my knife because I broke the tip off when I stuck it in the floor. And he says, what, that? No, it came that way so you can use it like a screwdriver.
<v Kerry Weeks>但我看了看他手里拿着的刀，看了看刀刃，看到刀尖是方的，就像它断了一样。我说，那就是我的刀，因为我把它扎在地板上时把刀尖弄断了。他说，什么，这个？不，它买来就是这样，你可以把它当螺丝刀用。

00:52:01.910 --> 00:52:05.530
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:52:05.530 --> 00:52:16.640
<v Kerry Weeks>And I look at the other boys and I'm not getting any help from them. And so I wander off, just thinking to myself, screwdriver. I never thought of that.
<v Kerry Weeks>我看着其他男孩，没得到任何帮助。所以我走开了，心里想，螺丝刀。我从没想过。

00:52:16.640 --> 00:52:19.600
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:52:19.600 --> 00:52:26.820
<v Kerry Weeks>Well, as you can imagine, this really bugged me. And I don't mean just while I was at camp, but for the rest of my life.
<v Kerry Weeks>嗯，正如你可以想象的那样，这真的困扰了我。我不只是指在夏令营期间，而是我余生都受此困扰。

00:52:26.820 --> 00:52:31.490
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:40.130
<v Kerry Weeks>And I kept thinking, why did I fold so easily? Why didn't I go to the counselors? And the only thing I can think of is this.
<v Kerry Weeks>我一直想，为什么我退缩得这么容易？为什么我不去找辅导员？唯一能想到的只有这个。

00:52:40.130 --> 00:53:01.430
<v Kerry Weeks>I'm the son of a physicist. And I used to ask my dad these questions all the time like, Dad, how far away is the sun? Dad, how fast is the speed of light? Dad, what's the closest star? And he would give me these answers and these numbers that were just so [BEEP] huge, I couldn't get my mind around them.
<v Kerry Weeks>我是物理学家的儿子。我过去常问我爸这些问题，比如，爸，太阳有多远？爸，光速有多快？爸，最近的恒星是什么？他会给出答案和一些数字，那些数字简直[哔]太巨大了，我根本无法理解。

00:53:01.430 --> 00:53:11.080
<v Kerry Weeks>So my point is this. I, better than most other children, had an idea of just how vast the universe is.
<v Kerry Weeks>所以我的观点是这个。我，比大多数孩子更有想法，知道宇宙是多么广阔。

00:53:11.080 --> 00:53:12.630
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:53:12.630 --> 00:53:42.450
<v Kerry Weeks>And therefore, how infinite the possibilities are. So the thing is, at the time, I thought, well, maybe the kid's telling the truth. The only reason why I bring this up is because this is what I was thinking of when, some 32 years later, I become the landlord of a small apartment building. And one of the first things I did as a landlord was I threw this guy out because he was a small time drug dealer.
<v Kerry Weeks>因此，可能性是无限的。所以事实是，当时我心想，好吧，也许那孩子说的是真话。我提起这件事的唯一原因是因为当32年后，我成为一栋小型公寓楼的房东时，我脑子里想的也是这个。我做房东做的第一件事就是把这家伙赶走，因为他是个小毒贩。

00:53:42.450 --> 00:53:55.640
<v Kerry Weeks>On the day he's moving out, I get a call from his neighbor across the hall-- a guy I call Budweiser Bob. And Budweiser Bob says, hey, that druggie is stealing your refrigerator.
<v Kerry Weeks>在他搬走那天，我接到了他隔壁邻居的电话——我叫他“百威鲍勃”。百威鲍勃说，嘿，那个瘾君子在偷你的冰箱。

00:53:55.640 --> 00:53:59.080
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:53:59.080 --> 00:54:06.570
<v Kerry Weeks>And so I hop down there. And sure enough, in the driveway is a pickup truck. And in the back of the pickup truck is a refrigerator.
<v Kerry Weeks>于是我冲了过去。果然，车道上一辆皮卡车。皮卡车后面是一个冰箱。

00:54:06.570 --> 00:54:26.070
<v Kerry Weeks>So I charge up the stairs and I demand to know just what the hell is going on. And Nick, the druggie, calmly tells me-- he says, dude, when I moved in here, that refrigerator was so gross, I bought my own refrigerator and swapped it out. And I put that one in storage. And now I'm just putting it back.
<v Kerry Weeks>所以我冲上楼梯，质问到底是怎么回事。尼克，那个瘾君子，冷静地告诉了我——他说，兄弟，我搬进来时，那冰箱太恶心了，我就买了台自己的冰箱换了上去。我把那台冰箱存起来了。现在我只是把它放回去。

00:54:26.070 --> 00:54:32.610
<v Kerry Weeks>And I look in the kitchen. And sure enough, there's another refrigerator. It's a little old, a little dirty, but it's working.
<v Kerry Weeks>我看了看厨房。果然，那里还有另一台冰箱。稍微旧了点，有点脏，但能用。

00:54:32.610 --> 00:54:52.320
<v Kerry Weeks>And I'm trying to think this through. Would someone really go to all that trouble of just swapping out a refrigerator and then putting it in storage and bringing it back? Or would a guy go to the trouble of stealing a used refrigerator just to screw his landlord and replacing it with an even more used refrigerator?
<v Kerry Weeks>我试着想清楚这件事。真的有人会费劲去换一台冰箱，然后存起来，再带回来吗？或者有人会费心去偷一台旧冰箱只是为了折磨他的房东，然后换上一台更旧的冰箱吗？

00:54:52.320 --> 00:54:55.530
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:54:55.530 --> 00:54:59.420
<v Kerry Weeks>And well, the answer to both these questions is yes.
<v Kerry Weeks>答案是，这两个问题的答案都是肯定的。

00:54:59.420 --> 00:55:02.900
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:55:02.900 --> 00:55:21.530
<v Kerry Weeks>But I look up, and there's Nick. And he's expecting me to say something. And there's his buddies, who are helping him move. And they're expecting me to say something. So I walk up to Nick and I look him in the eye and I say to him, you sweep up real good before you leave.
<v Kerry Weeks>但我也抬头看了，尼克在那儿。他等着我说什么。还有帮他搬家的伙伴们。他们等着我说什么。所以我走向尼克，看着他的眼睛说，你走之前把地打扫干净。

00:55:21.530 --> 00:55:23.190
<v Kerry Weeks>[LAUGHTER]
<v Kerry Weeks>[笑声]

00:55:23.190 --> 00:55:28.320
<v Kerry Weeks>Because there's just too many possibilities in this universe. Thank you.
<v Kerry Weeks>因为这个宇宙里的可能性实在太多了。谢谢。

00:55:28.320 --> 00:55:31.470
<v Kerry Weeks>[APPLAUSE]
<v Kerry Weeks>[掌声]

00:55:31.470 --> 00:55:46.410
<v Sarah Koenig>Kerry Weeks lives in New York. His story was recorded live at a Moth story slam in New York City. You can download the Moth podcast on iTunes or wherever you get your podcasts.
<v Sarah Koenig>凯瑞·威克斯住在纽约。他的故事是在纽约市的一次Moth故事大赛现场录制的。你可以在iTunes或其他获取播客的地方下载Moth播客。

00:55:46.410 --> 00:56:20.560
<v Sarah Koenig>At the beginning of the show, I said I was a skeptic. But after talking to so many people about coincidences-- including a Stanford mathematician who spent decades studying them-- I end up here, agreeing with this one woman I interviewed about her coincidence. She knew her story could probably be explained away with statistics and probability. But she said, there's just a poetry to things like this when they happen. There's some kind of beauty in it. There's meaning in the noticing of it at all.
<v Sarah Koenig>节目开始时，我说我是个怀疑论者。但在和这么多人聊过巧合之后——包括一位研究巧合几十年的斯坦福数学家——我最终得出了结论，同意我采访的那位女士对她巧合事件的评价。她知道她的故事大概可以用统计学和概率论解释掉。但她说，当这些事情发生时，其中有一种诗意。这其中有一种美感。即使只是注意到这些本身就很有意义。

00:56:20.560 --> 00:56:29.640
<v Sarah Koenig>[MUSIC - "CHILDREN OF COINCIDENCE" BY DORY PREVIN]
<v Sarah Koenig>[音乐 - "Children of Coincidence" by Dory Previn]

